\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Validation against the Memmingen measurements. \textbf  {Model} names the formulation each metric is evaluated against: the shipped KPIs are the \emph  {MILP baseline}, which uses a nominal constant return temperature and does not propagate the supply temperature. The cost conclusions depend on the annual delivered energy (hence network loss) and the heat-pump performance, both of which pass; the calibrated return temperature also matches within its ex-ante 2.5\,°C gate (bias only $-0.3$\,°C). The \emph  {supply}-temperature-propagation metrics (far-end, trunk drop, corridor) are \textbf  {n/a} for the MILP: their gates are defined for the non-linear (NLP) formulation only (\texttt  {VALIDATION\_METHODOLOGY.md}), and the MILP injects the source temperature as a boundary condition without propagating it -- so a zero simulated drop is by construction, not a fit failure. The physical temperature field is reproduced by the non-linear forward evaluator and anchored to the annual loss; point/corridor temperatures are additionally not validatable at this metering resolution (sensors downstream of mixing valves). Bias is signed mean error; scatter is RMSE.}}{6}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:validation}{{6}{6}{Validation against the Memmingen measurements. \textbf {Model} names the formulation each metric is evaluated against: the shipped KPIs are the \emph {MILP baseline}, which uses a nominal constant return temperature and does not propagate the supply temperature. The cost conclusions depend on the annual delivered energy (hence network loss) and the heat-pump performance, both of which pass; the calibrated return temperature also matches within its ex-ante 2.5\,°C gate (bias only $-0.3$\,°C). The \emph {supply}-temperature-propagation metrics (far-end, trunk drop, corridor) are \textbf {n/a} for the MILP: their gates are defined for the non-linear (NLP) formulation only (\texttt {VALIDATION\_METHODOLOGY.md}), and the MILP injects the source temperature as a boundary condition without propagating it -- so a zero simulated drop is by construction, not a fit failure. The physical temperature field is reproduced by the non-linear forward evaluator and anchored to the annual loss; point/corridor temperatures are additionally not validatable at this metering resolution (sensors downstream of mixing valves). Bias is signed mean error; scatter is RMSE}{table.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Exact loss$\times $topology decomposition of the copperplate-to-baseline cost gap on Memmingen (economic cost, all solves $\le $0.01\,\% gap; the additive identity closes to machine precision). The synthetic column reports the 135-network distribution.}}{6}{table.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:decomposition}{{7}{6}{Exact loss$\times $topology decomposition of the copperplate-to-baseline cost gap on Memmingen (economic cost, all solves $\le $0.01\,\% gap; the additive identity closes to machine precision). The synthetic column reports the 135-network distribution}{table.7}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.4}Secondary finding: generation topology as a moderator}{6}{subsection.4.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.5}Thermo-hydraulic effect, to the transmission station}{6}{subsection.4.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Estimation bias versus decision regret (\% of the baseline $\ensuremath  {\mathrm  {L1}}$ operating cost, forward evaluator), with the \emph  {decision} each model drove. Regret is shown for three shortfall-pricing schemes -- top-up at the marginal unit, the peak unit, and unmet demand penalised at the dump price. \emph  {Viol.} is physical-feasibility violations (velocity, differential pressure, unmet). The last two columns are the resulting dispatch: heat-pump energy share and running hours. The copperplate, blind to network loss, under-provisions the heat pump ($77.9$\,\%, 2063\,h) relative to the loss-aware node-resolved levels ($87.8$\,\%, $\sim $2310\,h), and pays the shortfall at the margin in winter hours it never planned for -- which is what the $+46$\,\% regret \emph  {is}, physically.}}{7}{table.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:regret}{{8}{7}{Estimation bias versus decision regret (\% of the baseline $\Lone $ operating cost, forward evaluator), with the \emph {decision} each model drove. Regret is shown for three shortfall-pricing schemes -- top-up at the marginal unit, the peak unit, and unmet demand penalised at the dump price. \emph {Viol.} is physical-feasibility violations (velocity, differential pressure, unmet). The last two columns are the resulting dispatch: heat-pump energy share and running hours. The copperplate, blind to network loss, under-provisions the heat pump ($77.9$\,\%, 2063\,h) relative to the loss-aware node-resolved levels ($87.8$\,\%, $\sim $2310\,h), and pays the shortfall at the margin in winter hours it never planned for -- which is what the $+46$\,\% regret \emph {is}, physically}{table.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Station-resolved hydraulics on Memmingen (real component data). The actual hydraulic pumping need is two orders of magnitude below the installed pump capacity, and every node keeps a large differential-pressure margin. Station-level hydraulics therefore move dispatch cost by $<1$\,\% and decisions by $\approx 0$.}}{7}{table.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:hydraulics}{{9}{7}{Station-resolved hydraulics on Memmingen (real component data). The actual hydraulic pumping need is two orders of magnitude below the installed pump capacity, and every node keeps a large differential-pressure margin. Station-level hydraulics therefore move dispatch cost by $<1$\,\% and decisions by $\approx 0$}{table.9}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.6}Robustness to a flexible supply temperature}{7}{subsection.4.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{subsec:tsup}{{4.6}{7}{Robustness to a flexible supply temperature}{subsection.4.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Forward supply-temperature sensitivity on $\ensuremath  {\mathrm  {L1}}$: lowering the plant supply temperature by $\Delta T_\text  {sup}$ cuts loss cost but raises pumping. Cost-optimal reduction marked $^{\star }$; the velocity limit binds at 20\,K. Loss valued at the marginal unit (upper bound). Costs in EUR/yr.}}{7}{table.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:tsup}{{10}{7}{Forward supply-temperature sensitivity on $\Lone $: lowering the plant supply temperature by $\Delta T_\text {sup}$ cuts loss cost but raises pumping. Cost-optimal reduction marked $^{\star }$; the velocity limit binds at 20\,K. Loss valued at the marginal unit (upper bound). Costs in EUR/yr}{table.10}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.7}Linearisation error and transport delay, separated}{7}{subsection.4.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{subsec:linearisation}{{4.7}{7}{Linearisation error and transport delay, separated}{subsection.4.7}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Extended-physics effects, each isolated (R1.3) and reported against its method and a rigorous bound (R2.3). Trunk pressure and the linearisation error are \emph  {solved}: pressure to a $0.01$\,\% gap, and the PWL-vs-native temperature error by a fix-and-relax nonlinear re-solve (the MILP schedule is re-costed under native, non-linearised temperature propagation on a winter and an autumn week, both converged to $\le 0.03$\,\% gap). Node temperature propagation is additionally reported through the validated nonlinear forward reference -- \emph  {not} its solved MILP, whose free-variable temperature form is degenerate (the optimiser floors the supply temperature for $\sim $91\,\% of hours, collapsing losses), consistent with \S  2.4/\S  4.7. Transport delay reproduces $\ensuremath  {\mathrm  {L3}}$ \emph  {identically} -- to the last digit ($+0.00$\,\%): trunk travel times are sub-hourly, so on the hourly grid the delay is a zero-timestep shift that storage absorbs. Every effect is far below the loss main effect, and the linearisation error is small \emph  {and negative} -- the PWL model slightly over-states cost.}}{8}{table.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:linearisation}{{11}{8}{Extended-physics effects, each isolated (R1.3) and reported against its method and a rigorous bound (R2.3). Trunk pressure and the linearisation error are \emph {solved}: pressure to a $0.01$\,\% gap, and the PWL-vs-native temperature error by a fix-and-relax nonlinear re-solve (the MILP schedule is re-costed under native, non-linearised temperature propagation on a winter and an autumn week, both converged to $\le 0.03$\,\% gap). Node temperature propagation is additionally reported through the validated nonlinear forward reference -- \emph {not} its solved MILP, whose free-variable temperature form is degenerate (the optimiser floors the supply temperature for $\sim $91\,\% of hours, collapsing losses), consistent with \S 2.4/\S 4.7. Transport delay reproduces $\Lthree $ \emph {identically} -- to the last digit ($+0.00$\,\%): trunk travel times are sub-hourly, so on the hourly grid the delay is a zero-timestep shift that storage absorbs. Every effect is far below the loss main effect, and the linearisation error is small \emph {and negative} -- the PWL model slightly over-states cost}{table.11}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.8}Secondary finding: generalisability and out-of-sample prediction}{8}{subsection.4.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{subsec:generalis}{{4.8}{8}{Secondary finding: generalisability and out-of-sample prediction}{subsection.4.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Balanced-design analysis of variance of the loss burden across the 135 synthetic networks (full $3\times 5\times 3\times 3$ factorial, so the sums of squares are unambiguous). Trunk pipe length dominates; the loss main effect is $\ge $97\,\% of the cost gap on every network regardless of factor, and the topology main effect stays within $\pm $2.4\,\%, so the decomposition conclusion is a property of the design, not of any one factor level.}}{9}{table.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:synthanova}{{12}{9}{Balanced-design analysis of variance of the loss burden across the 135 synthetic networks (full $3\times 5\times 3\times 3$ factorial, so the sums of squares are unambiguous). Trunk pipe length dominates; the loss main effect is $\ge $97\,\% of the cost gap on every network regardless of factor, and the topology main effect stays within $\pm $2.4\,\%, so the decomposition conclusion is a property of the design, not of any one factor level}{table.12}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Out-of-sample prediction of the cost gap (loss burden) from observable network properties. The estimator is fitted on the synthetic networks up to 15\,km trunk length (train $R^2=0.999$) and tested on the held-out longer networks, an \emph  {extrapolation} beyond the fitted range. It predicts the 30\,km networks to within a few points and degrades at the extreme 50\,km extrapolation (held-out MAPE 19\,\%), which we report as given.}}{9}{table.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:prediction}{{13}{9}{Out-of-sample prediction of the cost gap (loss burden) from observable network properties. The estimator is fitted on the synthetic networks up to 15\,km trunk length (train $R^2=0.999$) and tested on the held-out longer networks, an \emph {extrapolation} beyond the fitted range. It predicts the 30\,km networks to within a few points and degrades at the extreme 50\,km extrapolation (held-out MAPE 19\,\%), which we report as given}{table.13}{}}
